  [1mSection 2 — Notification rules and UI behavior[0m

  [1mThe problem I found: under the rules we agreed on, toasts almost never fire.[0m

  Actor exclusion says you're never notified about your own changes. But in this app there's exactly one active user at
  a time, and that user is always the actor. So when you're acting as Alice and edit a task Bob watches, the
  notification goes to [3mBob[0m — and Bob isn't looking. Alice sees nothing. The toast, as normally conceived, is dead code.

  The fix that keeps it useful: [1mtoast on catch-up, not just on arrival.[0m When you switch users, if the incoming user has
  unread notifications that arrived while they were away, toast a summary — "2 new notifications." Plus keep genuine
  arrival-toasts for the rare case where a recipient is the current user. That makes the switch the moment of payoff,
  which is exactly where the demo's drama lives anyway.

  [1mWhich changes notify[0m (subscribers = assignee ∪ creator ∪ watchers, always minus the actor):

  - Assigned / reassigned → the new assignee
  - Status changed → all subscribers
  - Title edited → all subscribers
  - Deleted → all subscribers
  - Created → the assignee, if it was assigned at creation
  - [1mWatch/unwatch notifies nobody[0m — it's a subscription action, not a change to the task

  [1mRead state:[0m the badge counts unread for the current user. Opening the panel does [3mnot[0m mark everything read — clicking a
  notification does, plus an explicit "mark all read." Auto-marking on open is the single most annoying pattern in real
  notification UIs and it would also make the demo harder to inspect.

  [1mDeliberately not building:[0m grouping, collapsing rapid repeat edits, mute, snooze, preferences. Edit a title three
  times and you get three notifications. That's honest and it's ten lines instead of a hundred; if it turns out to be
  irritating, that's a real finding and a good follow-up.

  [1mError handling[0m is narrow here: corrupt or unreadable localStorage falls back to seed data rather than crashing on
  boot, and an unrecognized event type renders a generic message instead of throwing. A demo that white-screens because

